# Line endings are load-bearing in this repository.
#
# dev-kit's hooks are bash scripts, and Git for Windows checks out with core.autocrlf=true by
# default — so without this file, `claude plugin install` on Windows lands a CRLF copy of
# hooks/session-start. A CRLF bash script does not degrade, it dies:
# `set -euo pipefail\r` is an "invalid option name", and the `-e` that was already applied
# turns that into an immediate exit. The hook then injects nothing, silently, and the whole
# thing looks like "the plugin just doesn't do anything on Windows".
#
# So: LF in the working tree, on every platform.
* text=auto eol=lf

# hooks/run-hook.cmd stays LF **deliberately**, even though it is a batch file, and this line
# is here to stop anyone "correcting" it to eol=crlf.
#
# It is a cmd/bash polyglot, and the bash half is the half that cannot survive CRLF: `fi\r`
# is an outright syntax error, and `SCRIPT_DIR="$(...)"\r` picks up a trailing CR that makes
# the exec'd path unopenable. cmd.exe, in contrast, reads LF-only batch files fine — the
# construct that genuinely breaks there is a label / `goto`, because cmd seeks to labels by
# byte offset. Hence the standing rule for run-hook.cmd: **no labels, no `goto`.**
*.cmd text eol=lf
*.bat text eol=lf

# Binaries, should any ever land here: never touch them. Evidence directories (screenshots,
# screen recordings) are gitignored in consuming projects, but templates and fixtures may
# eventually carry an image.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.mp4 binary
*.webm binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
